<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  place-recording must never DISCARD a take. This gates the planner branch for
  "the lane already holds a column that started BEFORE this recording did",
  driving the verb directly, so the whole case is arithmetic: no capture device,
  no transport, no wall-clock jitter anywhere in it.

  The bug: such a column cannot receive the recording as a take (a take is an
  alternative for the SAME window, so an earlier-starting column would need
  source material from before the recording began), so the plan loop skipped
  it; but it still advanced the planner's cursor past its own end, and so
  CONSUMED the recording material it covered. Cover the whole recording and the
  composite came out EMPTY, and an empty composite applies as SUCCESS, so the
  take vanished with only a qWarning to show for it.

  That is what made qxa.record_stays_armed fail about 1 run in 15: its two
  takes start a few thousand frames apart, because each record-start re-anchors
  its own placement conversion, so whether take 2 begins just before or just
  after take 1 is wall-clock jitter, and only the second spelling lost the
  audio. That case reaches this branch by accident; this one reaches it on
  purpose and on every run.

  test_sawtooth.wav is 4 s at 48 kHz = 192000 frames, which is what makes every
  number below a closed form.

  FLIPPED BY MODE A (plan 53 §9, QBX-180). The CLAIM is unchanged and is the
  reason the case exists: place-recording must never discard a take. What
  changed is how a surviving take is observed. The defect this gates was an
  EMPTY COMPOSITE - the pass was planned away and nothing was placed at all -
  and the old observable was "a new lane child appeared". Under mode A there
  is never a new lane child: every pass folds into ONE union column, so the
  observable is the column's TAKES and their EXTENTS, which say where each
  pass's material is, and a render that hears it.

  Three passes, each a closed form (the clip starts at 0 and the file is
  192000 frames, so take 0's extent is 0-192000 throughout):

    (1) [48000, 144000)   -> takes 2, extents 0-192000;48000-144000,
                             comp 0 -> clip, 48000 -> pass1, 144000 -> clip
    (2) [96000, 288000)   -> takes 3, the column grows to [0, 288000),
                             comp 0 -> clip, 48000 -> pass1, 96000 -> pass2
    (3) [48000, 144000)   -> takes 4, comp 0 -> clip, 48000 -> pass3,
                             144000 -> pass2

  THE OLD B5 ASSERTION IS STILL HERE in its own register. "The recording went
  to exactly ONE column" was asserted as `assert-take-column clip="0,2"
  shape="none"` - that a second overlapping child had NOT been wrapped into a
  column by accident. There is no second child to check any more; what the
  rule now means is that each pass appears as exactly ONE take, which is
  `takes=` on the one column: four takes for one clip and three passes, never
  five.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="place_recording_over_earlier_clip">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>

    <!-- The pre-existing column: [0, 192000). -->
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="192000"/>

    <!-- (1) TOTAL cover. The recording [48000, 144000) lies wholly inside the
         column above, so the buggy planner produced an empty composite and
         placed NOTHING. The take must survive, at its own start and its own
         full length. -->
    <place-recording trackPath="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav"
                     timePos="48000" length="96000"/>
    <!-- The pass survived, as a take over exactly its own span. Under the
         defect NOTHING was placed: the composite came out empty and applied
         as success, so there was no second take to find. -->
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="direct" takes="2" activeTake="1"
                        extents="0-192000;48000-144000"
                        comp="0:0;48000:1;144000:0" duration="192000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,1" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- (2) PARTIAL cover. The recording [96000, 288000) runs off the end of
         the column. The buggy planner placed only the uncovered tail, at
         192000 for 96000 frames, dropping the first half of the take. The
         whole take must be placed, from where it actually started. -->
    <place-recording trackPath="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav"
                     timePos="96000"/>
    <!-- The whole pass is there, from where it actually started: a take at
         96000-288000, and the column has grown to hold it. The buggy planner
         placed only the uncovered tail and dropped the first half. -->
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="direct" takes="3" activeTake="2"
                        extents="0-192000;48000-144000;96000-288000"
                        comp="0:0;48000:1;96000:2" duration="288000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,2" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- (3) THE TAKE-STACKING PATH IS UNTOUCHED, which is the half of this
         that a "just place it as a clip" fix would break. A recording starting
         exactly WITH an existing column still becomes a TAKE on it: column 0,1
         starts at 48000 and is 96000 long, so this recording covers it
         exactly. The observable is that NO fourth clip appears - had the take
         become a clip, 0,3 would resolve. -->
    <place-recording trackPath="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav"
                     timePos="48000" length="96000"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="direct" takes="4" activeTake="3"
                        extents="0-192000;48000-144000;96000-288000;48000-144000"
                        comp="0:0;48000:3;144000:2" duration="288000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,1" expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,3" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- ...AND THE RECORDING WENT TO EXACTLY ONE COLUMN (plan 53 B5, QBX-163).
         This section already REACHED the defect and walked past it: clip 0,2
         covers [96000, 288000), so it overlaps the column this pass just
         became a take on, and the plan loop handed it the SAME recording as a
         second take. Because add-take WRAPS a plain clip, 0,2 was silently
         turned into a take column.

         Asserting "no 0,3" could never see that - the wrap replaces a child in
         place and adds no fourth clip. The shape is the observable, and it is
         true both under the skip that fixes this and under P1's Q7 rule, so
         this assertion does not have to be rewritten when the planner is
         replaced. -->
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" takes="4"/>

    <!-- ...AND PASS (1) IS STILL AUDIBLE, which is the half no structural
         assertion can give. Comping to it gives its own material over its
         own span: source sec0 then sec1. Under the defect this span played
         the CLIP's material there (source sec1 then sec2), because nothing
         had been placed over it. -->
    <select-take clip="0,0" take="1"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" comp="0:0;48000:1;144000:2" activeTake="1"/>
    <render filename="prec_pass1.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.0"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="prec_pass1.wav" startFrame="48000"
                         frameCount="48000" minRms="0.05" maxRms="0.085"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="prec_pass1.wav" startFrame="96000"
                         frameCount="48000" minRms="0.15" maxRms="0.21"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
